{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target identifies 'differential longwave radiative cooling driven by emissivity and surface properties' as the single best explanation and claims the cross-city gap scales with impervious surface fraction. But the dominant nocturnal UHI mechanism is reduced longwave radiative loss from street canyons due to reduced sky view factor — a geometry effect, not material emissivity. The target's own countercondition concedes this: 'If material emissivity variations are dwarfed by sky view factor effects, this mechanism is secondary.' Urban materials typically have high emissivity (~0.90–0.95), close to vegetation and soil, so the 'lower emissivity' premise is empirically weak. Impervious surface fraction is an indirect proxy and does not capture the sky-view-factor reduction that is the principal reason canyon surfaces cool more slowly. By naming emissivity/thermal admittance and impervious fraction rather than sky view factor, the target misidentifies the single mechanism the problem asks for.","premise":null,"premise_evidence":[{"block":"target_src_001_mechanism","quote":"The lower emissivity of some urban materials compared to vegetation and soil can further slow radiative loss. The cross-city magnitude depends on impervious surface fraction and material emissivity: cities with more impervious, low-emissivity surfaces maintain a larger nocturnal temperature excess."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}